# factory1 — two suppliers feeding one factory (the assembly line)
# supplyA fills slowly (1/t), supplyB fast (3/t). the factory's RECIPE consumes
# one of each to make one unit of output. the recipe lives on the pathway, not on
# the edges — because its rate must read BOTH inputs at once, and an edge sees
# only one node. this is the first thing edges cannot express: a reaction.
#
# watch the bottleneck: the line runs only as fast as the SCARCER input
# (limiting). A is the slow supplier, so it rate-limits the factory at ~1/t —
# and B's bucket fills to the brim, the excess no recipe can keep up with.
node supplyA : range(0, 50), init(0) @ bucket(fill = water)   # raw material: fluid
node supplyB : range(0, 50), init(0) @ bucket(fill = water)
node factory : range(0, 50), init(0) @ box(fill = squares)    # finished units: a crate

external sourceA
external sourceB

sourceA -> supplyA : constant(c = 1)   # slow supplier: 1 unit / t
sourceB -> supplyB : constant(c = 3)   # fast supplier: 3 units / t

# the recipe: 1 A + 1 B -> 1 output, as fast as the scarcer input allows
pathway assemble : supplyA + supplyB -> factory : limiting(k = 0.5)
